Here's the full migration analysis and replacement code.

## Pattern Classification

| Code | Pattern | Problem on .NET 8 |
|---|---|---|
| `_thread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `_thread.Interrupt()` | Blocking call interruption | Obsolete (`SYSLIB0046`) |
| `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | Blocking call that must wake on stop | Won't respond to cancellation |
| Manual `Queue` + `lock` + `ManualResetEvent` | Producer-consumer | Replaced by `Channel<T>` |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _runTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        _runTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Expected on shutdown — don't propagate
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask EnqueueAsync(string item)
    {
        await _channel.Writer.WriteAsync(item);
    }

    // Synchronous overload if callers can't be made async
    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);
    }

    public async Task StopAsync()
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();        // Signal no more items
        _cts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); // Hard timeout
        if (_runTask is not null)
            await _runTask;
        _cts.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Migration Decisions

**`Channel<T>` replaces `Queue` + `lock` + `ManualResetEvent`** — it handles thread-safe queueing, blocking reads, and cancellation-aware waiting in one primitive. No manual locking needed.

**`CancellationToken` replaces `Interrupt`/`Abort`** — `ReadAllAsync(token)` exits immediately when the token fires. This is the cooperative cancellation model .NET 8 requires.

**Graceful + hard shutdown** — `Writer.Complete()` lets in-flight items drain. `CancelAfter(5s)` acts as the hard timeout, replacing the old `Join(5000)` → `Abort()` pattern.

## Pitfalls to Watch

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| **`Handle()` ignores cancellation** | If `Handle` does slow I/O, pass the token into it too — cancellation only works at checkpoints |
| **Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks internal timers. Always dispose after `StopAsync` |
| **Calling `Enqueue` after `Stop`** | `TryWrite` returns `false` after `Complete()`. Check the return value or guard with a state flag |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Only catch it in the top-level loop. Let it propagate from inner methods so the loop can exit |
| **Race between drain and cancel** | `Complete()` alone would drain all items. `CancelAfter` cuts it short. Choose which behavior you want — the current code mirrors the original's "best effort then kill" approach |
